Django 1.10 predates PEP 3135's __classcell__ becoming mandatory.

Python 3.6 warned when a metaclass dropped __classcell__ from the namespace;
Python 3.8 made it a RuntimeError. Django's ModelBase.__new__ builds the class
without propagating it, so every model definition fails with

    RuntimeError: __class__ not set defining 'AbstractBaseUser' ...
    Was __classcell__ propagated to type.__new__?

Django fixed this in 1.11. We are on 1.10.8 and not moving it here, so the
same fix is applied to the installed package. This has been carried on the
production host as an edit to site-packages that exists in no repository and
that nothing records; it is a patch file now so a machine can be rebuilt from
source without someone remembering.

--- a/django/db/models/base.py
+++ b/django/db/models/base.py
@@ -101,6 +101,14 @@
 
         app_label = None
 
+        # Propagate __classcell__ so Python 3.8 and later accept the class.
+        # Backported from Django 1.11; see patches/ for why this is here.
+        new_attrs = {'__module__': module}
+        classcell = attrs.pop('__classcell__', None)
+        if classcell is not None:
+            new_attrs['__classcell__'] = classcell
+        new_class = super_new(cls, name, bases, new_attrs)
+
         # Look for an application configuration to attach the model to.
         app_config = apps.get_containing_app_config(module)
 
